<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shreni">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shreni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE–12th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as śreṇi, seni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A shreni was an association of traders, merchants or artisans in ancient and early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, usually one for each vocation, which acted as an economic body, a largely self-governing community and, within limits, a tribunal for its members. Commonly translated as '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>', though scholars caution against the comparison with European guilds, shrenis are attested from the 6th century BCE to the 12th century CE and reached their height under the Mauryan and Gupta empires. Wealthy shrenis lent money and kept religious endowments; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> carvers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> are credited with sponsoring the southern gateway of the stupa at Sanchi. Their hereditary crafts may have fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system.</p></div>
